    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Looking at some examples, we see that putting in  $-1$  and  $1$ , we get  $f(-1) =0$  and  $f(1) = \\pi = \\lvert 0 \\rvert$ .  Thus,  $f(x) = 0$  for  $x \\in \\{-1,1\\}$ .  Letting  $u = \\arctan x$ ,  $f(x) = u + \\arctan \\frac{1 - u}{1 + u}$ .\n$\\frac{d}{du} f(x) = 0 + \\frac{1}{1 + u^2} = \\frac{1}{1 + u^2}$  so  $f(x)$  is strictly increasing, meaning that  $f(x)$  is the only solution and has one root.  $\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$\n\nBump.\n\nHow do you know that $f$ is strictly increasing?\n\nWolfram Alpha tells us $\\arctan x + \\arctan \\left(\\frac{1-x}{1+x}\\right) = \\arctan(x + 1) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ when $x \\ge 1$ and $0 \\le \\arctan (x - 1) \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ when $0 < x < 1$.\nAnd when $x \\le -1$, $\\arctan (x - 1) + \\arctan \\left(\\frac{1 + x}{1 - x}\\right) = \\arctan (-x - 1) = \\pi$.\nSo $\\arctan x + \\arctan \\left(\\frac{1-x}{1+x}\\right)$ is well-defined for all real $x$ and strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$ and strictly decreasing on $(-\\infty,0)$.\n\nThis is correct. In addition, we can also deduce that as $x \\rightarrow 0^{-}, f(x) \\rightarrow -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and as $x \\rightarrow 0^{+}, f(x) \\rightarrow \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. So the only acceptable value in radians is \\[\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}.\\]",
